s um which you mike  and james will might be able to help us  with but first  can i get a top tip for you on how to  save energy miko do you want to go first  i feel like a lot of people have already  been trying to do this over the past  year they've already done most of the  things that can be done you know turning  down thermostats wearing and wearing  extra jumpers martin lewis has said well  there isn't one  ultimately it's up to government at this  stage if you've got the money then  insulate your home the people who've got  the resources wonderful to put that into  insulating home to reducing and reducing  how much gets used but the majority  can't afford that at this stage  james what about you what's your top tip  um i think i'd echo that i mean it  depends on your circumstance if you have  the money the fact the way that bills  have gone up and the cut in v8t that's  just come into effect today and the new  grant scheme for heat pumps means that  all these technologies that are already  quite attractive are now really  financially attractive they'll save you  money quite quickly solar panels  insulation heat pumps so if you are  sitting on some savings you know you'll  get a better return on that than you  would sitting in a nicer and that  becomes a financially sensible and an  environmentally beneficial thing to do  but of course the tragedy of this  situation is millions and millions of  households do not have savings or they  might be living in homes where that's  not appropriate and that's why you're  seeing businesses civil society trade  unions and environmental groups all  saying exactly the same thing which is  the government needs to do more as  other governments have as we've seen the  french government the german government  comparable economies are trying to do  more to protect people from these price  rises absolutely such a worrying time  we'll get to that that that in a moment  but let's have a look at the first  question we have graham sent it in and  he has asked why have electricity prices  risen that the way they have when  suppliers say that it's from renewable  sources mika  so a lot of our electricity still comes  from gas and ultimately because of the  way our energy system is structured the  market way it's structured means that  even if your electricity is coming from  wind  the price is set by gas and that points  to how iron how our energy system as a  whole is bus that we're told for years  switch supplier and you'll get this  outcome and ultimately that was all nice  and good but right now in the crisis  whoever you've switched to pretty much  your bills are going up wherever it's  coming from and that means we need to  change the system as a whole and it's  fundamental change  we would say that public ownership  should play a significant role that  gives more more control more ability  we're seeing than a lot of the rest of  europe  we could have that here too  okay james what do you think to what  graham asked i mean much the same  unfortunately the way the market is  structured means that the gas price  effectively sets the price you can you  can t you can tweak it down a little bit  the more renewables you use but  ultimately the the unfortunately the gas  price and that's a wholesale gas price  an international gas price if you will  it doesn't matter how much we import  from russia compared to others  that shock will just feed through into  the system and it pushes up the price of  renewables as well as um gas so there  are people looking at this they're  looking at how we can structure the  market differently so that the the  lowest cost marginal form of power which  is almost invariably renewables can have  a bigger impact on the overarching price  but unfortunately um it's that that sort  of long-running work that takes a lot of  time and as we stand gas is setting the  price and gas is very high and very  volatile that's the other really  worrying thing here it could get worse  um if the ukraine situation continues to  deteriorate you could see further  pressure on prices right because the  current rise in prices doesn't even  reflect what's happened in ukraine does  it  no not as yet no i mean the worrying  thing is that the price cap's going to  get reviewed again in autumn it could go  higher still we're seeing the kremlin  saying they could turn off gas tax  exports to europe all together in  response to the sanctions we're seeing  the ukrainian government quite rightly  say please ramp up the sanctions to  include oil and gas and please could you  stop importing it because it is funding  uh putin's war machine so you know there  is a very real risk of it it going  higher and it depends on that  geopolitical context  mika when you say we need a fundamental  change in the market is that the  international market you're looking at  or the local market and how we're  structured in here how would you change  it and how long do you think it would be  to bring about that change  so i mostly mean in the uk because  that's where the government has the most  power and ultimately what we have now is  a market that was designed created  in the 80s and 90s and as i said it's  bust it's failed it's failed to deliver  secure and affordable energy  there's a significant role for public  ownership of energy instead of this  whole switch from one supplier to  another supplier actually having  supplies whether they're locally owned  by councils nationally providing that  affordable energy government can choose  to keep it the way it is which is  ultimately  serving mostly richer people or provide  a solution for everybody now that is  something that we need that's  transformational but you're right it  won't help tomorrow and that's why we'd  say the chancellor should come back now  with an emergency people's budget we  he's he did that during covert it's  perfectly feasible for him to come back  next week maybe we'll take him two weeks  come back we'v